y = 2\n- while granularity >= 2:\n    - try to remove chunks of size granularity from left to right, accept if required items still present.\n    - if any removal succeeded, set granularity = max(2, granularity - 1)? That doesn't match.\n\nGiven the ambiguous description, we need to infer from typical \"chunk reduction\" or \"delta debugging\" style? The problem mentions \"chunk_reduction_schedule\" and \"pass refinement\". It might be a known algorithm from program reduction like \"ddmin\" (delta debugging). ddmin starts with full list, then attempts to reduce by halves, etc. But here it's chunk-based removal at granularity, with restart after success and double after failure. That is reminiscent of \"coarsen granularity on failure, refine on success\" in some minimization algorithms. Possibly: \n- Start with chunk size = 2.\n- While True:\n    - For each chunk of that size (left to right), test if removing it preserves required items.\n    - If a removal succeeds, accept it, reset to the smallest chunk size? Or \"slightly coarser\" means increase chunk size by 1? Actually \"coarser\" means larger chunks. So if a removal succeeds, we want to try even larger chunks next? That seems odd: if removal succeeded, we might want to try finer granularity to see if smaller chunks can be removed? But the description says \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" - that means after a successful removal, we increase the chunk size a little (e.g., +1). Then \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" means when we cannot remove any chunk of current size, we increase chunk size by doubling. But if we double after failure, then after success we increase by a little, so the overall effect is that we explore all granularities, but after success we stay in the same region. This seems like a search that gradually increases granularity, but if we fail at a certain size, we jump to much larger size. That might be designed to quickly find large removable