be we break the loop and then check one_minimal.\n        break\n\nAfter loop, need to compute one_minimal: check if for any i from 0 to len(items)-1, removing that single item (i.e., chunk size 1) would still keep all required items. If no single item can be removed, then one_minimal = True. Otherwise False. This matches the typical definition: a set is one-minimal if no element can be removed without violating the property.\n\nBut note: the algorithm might have already tried to remove single items if g ever became 1. Since we only start at 2 and increase, we never try single items. But at the end we can test one_minimal.\n\nLet's test on the first fixture: items = t0..t9, required = {t0, t6}. Let's simulate:\n- g=2:\n  - i=0: trial = items[2:] = [t2..t9], required still contains? t0 is removed, so fail.\n  - i=1: trial = [t0] + items[3:], required: t0 present, t6 present? t6 is at index 6 in original, after removal of indices 1-2, new list: indices 0:t0, then from index3: t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. So t6 present. So removal of chunk [t1,t2] works. Accept. items = [t0, t3, t4, t5, t6, t7, t8, t9]. attempts=1. g becomes 3.\n  - Now g=3, list length 8.\n    - i=0: trial = items[3:] = [t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] - missing t0 -> fail.\n    - i=1: trial = items[:1] + items[4:] = [t0] + [t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] = missing t3? Actually t3 is removed. Required {t0,t6} still present? t0 present, t6 present? t6 is at index? In new list: indices:0:t0, then t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. t6 present. So removal of chunk of size 3 starting at i=1 (items[1:4] = [t3,t4,t5]) works. Accept. items = [t0, t4, t5, t6, t7, t8, t9]. attempts=2. g=4.\n    - g=4, list length 7.\n      - i=0: trial = items[4:] = [t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] - missing t0 -> fail.\n      - i=1: trial = items[:1] + items[5:] = [t0] + [t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] = missing t4? Actually removed items[1:5] = [t4,t5,t6,t7]? Wait, i=1, chunk = items[1:5] = indices1,2,3,4 = t4,t5,t6,t7. Required includes t6, which is in the chunk. After removal, t6 is gone, so fai